{"description":"Priority: P2 | Area: agent | Labels: test,area:agent,P2 | Milestone: v0.1.0 | RFC: none | Task: AI-0078

AIQ-44 (cache invalidation granularity) is needs-evidence: stale inputs must not produce a falsely current PASS. The runtime has no cache layer, but context assembly reuses records across rounds within a turn (tool_history, assembled records, artifact references) - the invalidation-relevant surface is: superseded records never resurface, rotated generations invalidate prior-tier grants, and artifact references resolve to stored bytes or fail closed. TDD: write tests FIRST in crates/bitty-ai-runtime/tests/ (new file cache_invalidation.rs), implement only if a test exposes a gap.

Required proofs (doubles only, deterministic): superseded L1 records do not reappear in later-round assembly; a mid-turn rotate_generation downgrade invalidates remaining dispatches (S-1, partially covered - extend the untouched half: grant invalidation visibility, not just denial); artifact store full/evicted references fail closed rather than serving stale bytes; re-assembled context after tool_history growth keeps stable-prefix bytes for unchanged heads (prefix-stability, no false-current claim). Scope: tests plus context.rs/session.rs/agent.rs ONLY if a gap is proven. No AIQ status change (narrowing input only). Evidence bar: cargo test/clippy/fmt green, review APPROVE.","displayId":"AI-0078","id":"01M2MF90GH1H6T1KJSJJNQQDXX","priority":"normal","status":"ready","title":"test(cache): cache-invalidation granularity denial proof (AIQ-44 narrowing)"}

{"description":"Priority: P2 | Area: agent | Labels: area:testing,area:agent,P2 | Milestone: v0.1.0 | RFC: none | Task: AI-0083

AIQ-47 (observability cost) is needs-evidence: the per-turn cost ceiling (AI-0046) bounds spend, not diagnostic subscription rate/priority. The runtime has no diagnostics bus; what can be proven offline is that cost accounting itself stays bounded and non-allocating under adversarial load, i.e. the accounting path cannot become the unbounded cost it meters. TDD: tests FIRST in crates/bitty-ai-runtime/tests/ (new file accounting_bounds.rs), minimal production change ONLY if a test proves a gap.

Required proofs (doubles only, deterministic): turn_cost saturates (no u64 overflow) under maxed weights and token counts (saturating_add already used - prove it with u32::MAX weights and u16::MAX-ish token counts); estimate_cost is pure and allocation-free in a loop (N repeated calls, assert identical results and no growth observable via repeated timing-free determinism - assert value stability, not wall time); CostCeilingExceeded fires before dispatch with exact limit/actual fields under adversarial weights; zero-weight uncalibrated rule (0 counts as 1) holds at the routing estimate AND turn accounting with a shared assertion. Scope: tests plus selection.rs/agent.rs ONLY if a gap is proven. No AIQ status change (narrowing input only). Evidence bar: cargo test/clippy -D warnings/fmt green, review APPROVE.","displayId":"AI-0083","id":"01M2MQ1GNQCG39PD5DKA2TVCW2","priority":"normal","status":"ready","title":"test(diagnostics): bounded subscription rate evidence for the cost router (AIQ-47 narrowing)"}

{"description":"Priority: P2 | Area: agent | Labels: area:testing,area:agent,P2 | Milestone: v0.1.0 | RFC: none | Task: AI-0090

AIQ-08 (MCP schema cache invalidation) is needs-evidence: stale schemas cannot authorize changed effects. The runtime has no schema-version source (MCP transport is host-owned and stubbed), but the ToolRegistry DOES hold schema_json per spec and authorizes dispatches against it - the provable surface is: a dispatch authorized against schema version N must not silently execute when the registered schema for that tool has changed to version N+1, and the registry must expose a deterministic schema-identity mechanism so a host-side version source can invalidate.

TDD: tests FIRST in crates/bitty-ai-runtime/tests/ (new file schema_invalidation.rs), minimal production change ONLY if a test proves a gap. Candidate mechanism (implementer verifies against code, adapts if the shape differs): deterministic schema digest (FNV-1a-64 over schema_json, mirroring cache_key.rs precedent - no new deps) exposed as ToolSpec::schema_digest plus a registry re-registration rule (same name plus different digest = explicit replace-with-evidence: old dispatches in flight fail closed, new dispatches bind the new digest; OR same-name-different-bytes refused with DuplicateTool - whichever the code already does, prove it; if neither exists, implement the fail-closed replace rule minimally). Proofs: digest determinism + sensitivity; stale-dispatch denial (authorize-then-replace-then-dispatch fails); in-flight calls bound to the digest they were authorized against. Deterministic doubles only, static assert messages (CodeQL lesson). Scope: tests plus tool.rs ONLY if a gap is proven. No MCP transport work (host-owned). No AIQ status change (narrowing input only). Evidence bar: cargo test/clippy -D warnings/fmt green, review APPROVE. After merge: sync the evidence into the triage doc AIQ-08 row pointer (docs task, same pattern as CTX-0054).","displayId":"AI-0090","id":"01M2N6SCTP4PMHT6A1ZWEFT5ZB","priority":"normal","status":"ready","title":"test(schema): stale-schema denial proof for the tool registry (AIQ-08 narrowing)"}

{"description":"Priority: P2 | Area: agent | Labels: area:testing,area:agent,P2 | Milestone: v0.1.0 | RFC: none | Task: AI-0092

AIQ-2C (Interactive writer fencing) is needs-evidence: single-threaded !Send session contract (AI-0062) documents one in-process fence; interactive writer fencing across takeover/restart has no mechanism. The runtime is single-process sans-I/O, so cross-process fencing cannot be built - but the FENCING RULE can be proven at the seam, extending the AI-0091 fence_token pattern: takeover/restart must invalidate stale writers before new input. TDD: tests FIRST in crates/bitty-ai-runtime/tests/ (new file writer_fencing.rs), minimal production surface ONLY if a test proves a gap.

Candidate rule to pin (implementer verifies against session.rs/adoption.rs, adapts if the shape differs): a WriterLease { writer_id, session_generation, epoch } plus check_writer(lease, current session state, current epoch/generation) that (a) refuses writes from a lease whose generation != current session generation (restart invalidates: new run_turn generation bumps, old writer handles die), (b) refuses writes from a lease whose epoch != current epoch (supervisor-epoch fencing, same StaleEpoch shape as adoption.rs), (c) refuses writes against terminal sessions (AlreadyTerminated), (d) a takeover operation explicitly revokes all outstanding leases (generation bump with proof that pre-takeover leases all refuse afterwards). Proofs: stale-generation write refused; stale-epoch write refused; terminal-session write refused; post-takeover old leases all refused with exact lease set; fresh lease after takeover succeeds. Deterministic, no threads/processes/IO/clock, static assert messages (CodeQL lesson). Scope: tests plus a small new module or session.rs extension ONLY if a gap is proven. No cross-process mechanism (state explicitly). No AIQ status change (narrowing input only). Evidence bar: cargo test/clippy -D warnings/fmt green, review APPROVE. After merge: triage-doc AIQ-2C evidence sync (docs task, no status change).","displayId":"AI-0092","id":"01M2NGN5S1SF55XCP8VKRSD93R","priority":"normal","status":"ready","title":"test(fencing): interactive writer-fencing rule with stale-writer invalidation (AIQ-2C narrowing)"}

